text:
nora: heads-up — Rust quickstart broke our onboarding smoke tests. The sample crate and example code are out of date, so users copying the README get compile errors right away.

peter: what drift hit us?

nora: three small changes cascaded:
1) crate name changed from `redwood-sdk` -> `redwood` in Cargo.toml samples, so `cargo add redwood-sdk` fails/resolves to old package.
2) env var renamed from `REDWOOD_API_KEY` to `REDWOOD_TOKEN` (same theme as other langs). README still shows the old name.
3) client API moved from `Redwood::new(api_key)` to a builder pattern `Client::builder().token(...).build()` and the generate function now takes `GenerateOptions` instead of a bare &str. The example panics at compile time with `no function or associated item named 'new' found` and then runtime missing token.

ivy: ouch. Any snippet output from the runner?

nora: snippet-runner logs: ``error[E0599]: no associated function named `new` found for struct `Redwood` in the current scope`` then later ``thread 'main' panicked at 'missing token'`` depending on which check ran first.

peter: can you paste the broken README block?

nora: sure — broken README snippet: ```rust
use redwood_sdk::Redwood;
use std::env;

fn main() {
  let api_key = env::var("REDWOOD_API_KEY").expect("set REDWOOD_API_KEY");
  let client = Redwood::new(api_key);
  let res = client.generate("hello from rust");
  println!("{}", res.text);
}
```

ivy: ok that explains it. propose corrected snippet and a small note about env renames and crate name.

nora: suggested corrected snippet: ```rust
// Cargo.toml: add `redwood = "^1.0"`
use redwood::Client;
use std::env;

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> anyhow::Result<()> {
    // env var renamed in v1.0: REDWOOD_TOKEN
    let token = env::var("REDWOOD_TOKEN").expect("set REDWOOD_TOKEN");

    let client = Client::builder().token(token).build()?;

    let opts = redwood::GenerateOptions { prompt: "hello from rust".into(), ..Default::default() };
    let resp = client.generate(opts).await?;
    println!("{}", resp.text);
    Ok(())
}
```

peter: looks good. also add a small note showing how to set env on mac/linux (`export REDWOOD_TOKEN=...`) and Windows PowerShell. we keep hitting that with newcomers.

ivy: can we also add a CI check that runs `cargo check` on extracted Rust snippets? We run compile checks for Go/Node/Python but not for Rust yet. Adding rust compile/check would have caught this at the docs PR.

nora: agreed — I'll open a PR to update quickstart-rust.md, fix crate name, env var, and example. i'll also add a one-line migration note and a follow-up issue to wire up Rust snippet compile in the snippet-checker.

ci-bot: @nora opened PR #1347 updating quickstart-rust.md. Snippet-runner (non-compile) passed; created issue #1402 to add `cargo check` to docs CI.

docs-bot: merged PR #1347. snippet-checker will be updated once #1402 is implemented.

peter: nice catch — this was confusing new users. Thank you for the quick fix. :tada:
